  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 136 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nForcing is expected to weaken Saturday as the wave moves \neastward, but the general trend has been towards a bit slower exit\nfor precipitation chances Saturday, with rain showers potentially\nlasting into the afternoon or early evening across central and \nnorth-central OK. Once again lightning potential appears low. \nStorm-total rainfall amounts are likely to exceed an inch across \nportions of western OK and western north TX where NBM is still \nhighlighting over 60-70% probabilities of this amount, with \nprobabilities of exceeding an inch of rain quickly dropping to \n10-20% along and east of I-35 where totals closer to 0.5-0.75\" are\nexpected to be more common.\n\nDrier conditions are forecast Saturday night into much of the day\non Sunday but will not last long as another system is slated to \nmove in rather quickly behind this one. Models are still \nstruggling to resolve the details of this system as energy from \nthe northern and southern streams attempts to phase over the \ncentral US. Depending on how the two streams interact will \ndetermine the track and amplitude of the system as it moves across\nthe area early next week. The more amplified solutions would \nbring a risk for severe weather and/or heavy rainfall to portions \nof the area, while other solutions show extensive dry-slotting and\nmuch lower rainfall totals. No clear favorite is evident in the \nensemble members at the moment leading to rather low forecast \nconfidence during this period, though for now the period of \ngreatest potential impact appears to be centered around Monday. \nCooler temperatures are then forecast to move in behind this \nsystem, bringing at least a brief break from our above average \nstretch by the middle of next week. \n\nWare",
